/**
@defgroup path read a CBOR value by its JSONPath (RFC 9535)

`cbor::at_path` reads one value from CBOR bytes by its JSONPath (RFC
9535), with no decode of the rest. A path also reads CBOR that JSON does
not have: keys that are not text, tags and typed arrays. A path that
selects many nodes gives a `cbor::nodelist`. Include `<cbor/cbor.hpp>`.

A path is a string literal that the compiler checks, or a string that
`cbor::path::compile` parses once at run time. Both read the same
grammar. The call reads the bytes in place. It does not copy the bytes
and keeps nothing. It reads the heads from the start of the item to the
target and no byte after the target.

@section path_forms THE FORMS

A path at compile time is a string literal. A path that is not valid, or
that selects many nodes where one node is expected, does not compile. The
type of the literal is `cbor::fixed_string`. A template of the program
names it to pass a path on.

- `cbor::at_path<Path, T>(encoded)`: `encoded` is a `std::string_view`,
  so a `std::string` or one element of a `cbor::sequence` goes in
  directly. For a number, a truth value, `std::nullptr_t` or
  `cbor::simple_value`, it gives `std::expected<T, cbor::error>`. For
  `std::string` or `std::vector<std::byte>`, it copies the text or the
  bytes of the value.
- `cbor::at_path<Path, T>(owner)`: `T` is a view type, and `owner` is a
  `std::shared_ptr<std::string const>`. It gives
  `std::expected<cbor::ref<T>, cbor::error>`.
- `cbor::at_path<Path, T>(owner, view)`: `owner` is a
  `std::shared_ptr<void const>`, and `view` is a borrowed range of `char`
  that `owner` keeps alive. The call does not check that the owner holds
  the bytes.
- `cbor::at_path<Path, T>(std::move(encoded))`: the rvalue
  `std::string` moves into a new owner.
- `lazy.at_path<Path, T>()` reads from a `cbor::lazy`, where `$` is the
  value of the lazy. `lazy.at_path<Path>()` gives the node as a
  `cbor::lazy`. `lazy.at_path<Path, cbor::nodelist>()` gives the
  nodelist.
- `cbor::path::compile<T>` parses the text of a path at run time and
  gives a `cbor::path::query<T>`. The query owns its data, and one query applies
  to any number of items. An apply does not parse again.
- `cbor::at_path(encoded, q)`, `cbor::at_path(owner, q)`,
  `cbor::at_path(owner, view, q)`, `cbor::at_path(std::move(encoded), q)`
  and `lazy.at_path(q)` apply a query. The result type is that of the
  forms above, with the `T` of the query.

No free form takes a lazy. A lazy reads a path with its member
`at_path`. `cbor::schema<T>::at_path` reads a member of a message that
`cbor::schema<T>` wrote. Its path names members and indexes only.

`T` of a view is `std::string_view`, `std::span<std::byte const>` or
`cbor::typed_array`. A view needs an owner of the bytes. A view type with
a `std::string_view` alone does not compile. A `cbor::ref` compares and
reaches its value as `std::optional` does. `cbor::ref<cbor::typed_array>`
has no `==`.

@section path_types THE TYPES OF A READ

- An integer type, also `char`: an integer, or a tag 2 or 3 whose
  magnitude fits in 64 bits. A value out of range gives
  `number_out_of_range`.
- `double`: a half, single or double float. It also reads an integer or a
  bignum that a `double` holds exactly: an integer from -2^53 to 2^53,
  and a larger one such as 2^60 or -2^64 whose bits from the highest 1 to
  the lowest 1 fit in 53 bits. Another integer gives `number_out_of_range`.
  A float is not read as an integer.
- `bool`: `true` or `false`.
- `std::nullptr_t`: `null`.
- `cbor::simple_value`: any simple value, also `false`, `true` and
  `null`.
- `std::string_view`: a text string.
- `std::span<std::byte const>`: a byte string.
- `cbor::typed_array`: a tag of RFC 8746 over a byte string.

Another kind of item gives `incorrect_type`. No other conversion exists.
`float` and any other `T` do not compile. For any other result, read the
node with `lazy.at_path<Path>()`.

@section path_syntax THE SYNTAX

The grammar is RFC 9535, with EDN literals (draft-ietf-cbor-edn-literals)
in brackets and in filters.

- `$` is the whole value. `$.a` and `$['a']` select a name. A name in dot
  form starts with a letter, `_` or a byte of 0x80 or more, and goes on
  with these or digits. A name in brackets is in single or double quotes,
  with the escapes of RFC 9535 section 2.3.1 and of EDN.
- `$[0]` is the first element and `$[-1]` the last. An index is an
  integer of at most 16 digits without leading zeros, from -(2^53 - 1) to
  2^53 - 1. An integer of 16 digits or less outside this range, and `$[01]`,
  give `invalid_path`.
- `$[*]` and `$.*` select every element of an array and every value of a
  map. `$..a`, `$..*` and `$..[0]` select at any depth.
- `$[0, 2]` has several selectors. `$[start:end:step]` is a slice. Each
  part of a slice can be absent. Step 0 selects nothing. A slice of a
  node that is not an array selects nothing.
- `$[?test]` is a filter, and `@` is the current value. A test is a
  comparison, an existence test such as `@.isbn`, or a function. `&&`
  binds more tightly than `||`. `!` negates an existence test or a group
  in parentheses. A filter can use an absolute query that starts at `$`.
- A literal in a filter is an EDN literal. A JSON number, a string,
  `true`, `false` and `null` are EDN literals too.
- A blank is allowed before a segment and inside brackets. A blank before
  `$` or after the last segment gives `invalid_path`.

A name selects only from a map. An index selects from an array, and from
a map with an integer key.

@section path_filters COMPARISONS AND FUNCTIONS

A comparison is `==`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>` or `>=`. Each side is a
singular query, a literal or a function. A side that is a query that is
not singular gives `invalid_path`, for example `$[?@.* == 1]`.

- Two numbers compare by value, so 1 equals 1.0. Two texts compare by
  their bytes.
- Two byte strings, two arrays, two maps and two tags can be equal and
  are never less. Arrays are equal element by element, in order. Maps are
  equal when they have the same keys with equal values. Tags are equal
  when they have the same number and equal content.
- A bignum (tag 2 or 3) with a magnitude up to 64 bits is an integer in a
  comparison (RFC 8949, section 3.4.3). A bignum with a larger magnitude
  is a tag.
- Values of two different kinds are not equal.
- A side that selects nothing is equal only to another side that selects
  nothing, and is never less. `<=` is true when `<` or `==` is true.

The functions are `length`, `count` and `value` (RFC 9535, sections
2.4.4, 2.4.5 and 2.4.8).

- `length(x)` gives, for a text, the count of its bytes that are not
  UTF-8 continuation bytes (0x80 to 0xBF), for an array its elements, and
  for a map its pairs. For a text in valid UTF-8, this is the count of
  characters. It gives Nothing for any other value, a byte string
  included.
- `count(q)` gives the number of nodes in the nodelist of `q`.
- `value(q)` gives the value of the node when `q` has one node, and
  Nothing otherwise.

Nothing is the result of a function that has no value (RFC 9535, section
2.4.1). Nothing equals Nothing and no other value. A function that is not
well-typed (RFC 9535, section 2.4.3) gives `invalid_path`.

`match`, `search` and I-Regexp are not supported. A path with `match` or
`search` gives `invalid_path` at run time and does not compile at
compile time. Function extensions beyond the three above are not
supported. Normalized paths (RFC 9535, section 2.7) are not given: a
nodelist holds values, not their places.

@section path_nodelist SINGULAR QUERIES AND NODELISTS

A singular query (RFC 9535, section 2.3.5.1) has a name or an index in
each segment, and no descendant segment. It selects one node at most. An
EDN key in brackets is a name with one key, so a path with it is
singular.

`cbor::is_valid_path_v<Path>` is true when the grammar accepts the path.
`cbor::is_singular_query_v<Path>` is true when the path is valid and
singular.

`at_path` with a typed result or a node reads a singular query only.
`lazy.at_path<Path>()` with another path stops at a `static_assert` that
names `cbor::nodelist`. `cbor::path::compile<T>` with such a path and a
`T` that is not `cbor::nodelist` gives `not_singular_query`.

A path that selects many nodes reads only into a `cbor::nodelist`: a
wildcard, a descendant segment, a slice, a filter or several selectors.
A `cbor::nodelist` is a `std::vector<cbor::lazy>`. It gives every match
as a `cbor::lazy` that holds the owner of the bytes. It also reads a
singular path. A path that selects nothing gives an empty nodelist and
no error. A node can be any CBOR item: a map, an array or a tag. Read it
on with a step, `get`, `type()` or `clone()`.

The compiler parses a compile-time path once. At run time it is a set of
constant arrays. A run-time path is checked once, in `compile`: the
grammar, the singular path for a `T` that is not `cbor::nodelist`, and
the segments against the nesting depth. The type `T` of a typed read is
checked at compile time in both. Everything about the data is checked at
each call.

@section path_cbor CBOR BEYOND JSON

An index on a map selects the value under the equal integer key, as COSE
(RFC 9052) uses it. `$[1]` finds the key 1 and `$[-2]` finds the key -2.
This is not an index from the end: a map has no order in a path.

An EDN literal in brackets is a key. `$[h'01']` is a byte string,
`$[[1, 2]]` an array, `$[1.5]` a number with a fraction, `$[true]`, and
`$[1000("x")]` a tag. This holds at compile time and at run time. On an
array, such a key gives `not_indexable`.

Keys compare by value (RFC 8949, section 5.6.1). So `h'01'` and
`b64'AQ'` name the same key, and a key with a head that is not in
preferred form is the same key. An integer and a float are different
keys: `$[1.0]` does not find the key 1. `$[-0]` is the key 0, and an
integer of 17 digits or more is a key.

When a map has the same key twice, every singular form takes the first
pair. A wildcard, a descendant segment and a filter take all pairs.

A path passes a tag that it does not read, as a generic decoder does (RFC
8949, section 6.1). A step and a typed read go to the content. A path
that ends at the tag gives the node of the whole tagged item, so the
caller reads the tag number.

- Tag 24 (encoded CBOR data item): the path goes into the byte string and
  reads the embedded item.
- Tag 28 (shareable): the path passes it. The value is the content.
- Tag 29 (shared reference): the path passes it as any other tag. The
  value is the content, the index. A path does not resolve the
  reference. `cbor::decode` does.
- Tag 55799 (self-described CBOR): the path passes it at the top, inside
  the item and inside a tag 24. It does not pass it as the content of a
  tag 2, a tag 3 or a typed array. A map key behind a tag 55799 is
  matched as the key without the tag. A key behind any other tag, a tag
  28 included, is a different key.
- Tags 2 and 3 (bignum): the path does not pass them. A typed read into an
  integer type reads the magnitude, and an index on a bignum gives
  `not_indexable`.
- A tag of RFC 8746 (typed array): an index reads one element. A wildcard,
  a slice or a filter gives every element. A typed read into
  `cbor::typed_array` reads the whole array.
- Any other tag: the path passes it. The value is the content.

An element of an integer array is an integer. An element of a float array
is a float of the same width. An element of a clamped uint8 array (tag
68) is an unsigned integer. An element of a 128-bit float array (tags 83
and 87) gives `incorrect_type`. An index on an element of a typed array
gives `not_indexable`.

@section path_limits LIMITS, COST AND THREADS

The limits are the constants of `cbor::limits`.

- `string_length`: every string that the walk reads is checked, that is a
  key that it compares and the target. A string that it only skips does
  not count.
- `container_elements`: every array and map that the walk reads is
  checked, that is the containers on the way and the target. A container
  that it only skips does not count.
- `nesting_depth_exceeded` comes where the stack of the thread ends, in
  the steps that recurse: a filter, a group in parentheses, a function
  call or a literal that is nested deeper than the stack holds, a
  descendant segment that goes deeper than the stack holds, and `clone()`
  of a node that is deeper than the stack holds. A path of child segments
  does not recurse. An item that a typed read only skips can be of any
  depth.

The limits count only what a path or a lazy reads or decodes. A skip
jumps over the content of a string, and it stops with `too_little_data`
when the item claims more than the bytes that are left.

A path does not decode what it passes. In a map, it reads each key until
the key matches and skips each value before it. In an array, `[i]` skips
the i elements before the target, so an index is linear in the bytes of
the elements before it. `[-1]` skips all elements except the last. A key
that is a map or an array compares by its items and its pairs.

A typed read of a scalar allocates nothing, also through a tag 24 and
with a key that is not text. A view result from an owner allocates
nothing: the owner in the result is a copy of a `std::shared_ptr`. The
form with an rvalue `std::string` allocates the `std::shared_ptr` that
holds the string, once. `cbor::path::compile` allocates. A nodelist read
allocates the nodelist. A filter with a literal allocates for each child
that it tests. An element of a typed array is read as a small CBOR item
that the library writes, and this allocates once for each step.

A held `cbor::lazy` is a start point. A path from a lazy at `$.cars` does
not read the bytes before `cars` again, so many reads below one node are
cheaper from a lazy at that node. Several threads read one `cbor::lazy`
at the same time. `cbor::at_path<Path, T>(encoded)` has no shared state.

A path reads the bytes up to its value and no further, so it does not
find a fault after the value. An array or a map that claims more items
than the bytes left gives `too_little_data` before any element is read.
`cbor::lazy::adopt` and `cbor::lazy::view` do not read the whole item
either. `cbor::item_size` and `cbor::decode` check a whole item.

A run-time `cbor::nodelist` query gives the result of the JSONPath
Compliance Test Suite in 694 of 706 cases. 50 of these are valid paths
with `match` or `search`, and the library refuses them. The other 12 are
invalid in RFC 9535 and valid EDN, so a path accepts them: a number such
as `+1`, `.1`, `-.1`, `1.` or `1.e1` in a filter, a key such as `$[1.0]`,
`$[+1]` or `$[-0]`, a name with a quote such as `$["\'"]`, and a name
with the escape `\u{1234}` or `\u{10ffff}`. This library adds to RFC
9535 an index selector that finds an integer key, and EDN literals as
keys and in filters.

@section path_errors ERRORS

Each function gives `std::expected` with a `cbor::error`. A missing field
and broken input have different errors, so a program tells them apart.

- `invalid_path`: the path text is not valid, or it calls `match` or
  `search`, or a comparison has a side that is not singular, or an index
  of 16 digits or less is outside the range of an index.
- `not_singular_query`: `cbor::path::compile<T>` with a path that is not
  singular and a `T` that is not `cbor::nodelist`.
- `key_not_found`: a name or a key is not in the map.
- `index_out_of_bounds`: the index is outside the array.
- `not_indexable`: a name, an index or a key meets a value that is not a
  map or an array, for example a text, a number, a bignum or an element of
  a typed array. A name or a key meets an array or a typed array.
- `incorrect_type`: a typed read finds another kind of item than `T`
  takes.
- `number_out_of_range`: the integer does not fit `T`.
- `nesting_depth_exceeded`: the path or the item is nested deeper than the
  stack of the thread holds.
- `string_length_exceeded`, `container_elements_exceeded`: a limit is set
  and the input goes past it.
- `too_little_data`: the bytes end before the target or before an item
  that the walk reads.
- `syntax_error`, `indefinite_length`: a head that the walk reads is not
  well-formed, or uses indefinite length.
- `inadmissible_type_for_tag_content`: a tag on the way has content that
  its definition does not allow, for example tag 24 over a number.
- `nodelist_too_long`: one step of a nodelist read selects more nodes
  than the top-level item has bytes.
- `not_enough_memory`: the allocation of the owner fails, in the form
  with an rvalue.

A wrong use that the compiler cannot see throws `std::logic_error`: a
null or empty owner in a form that takes an owner, a moved-from query, and
a `cbor::lazy{}` that holds no item in every form that takes a lazy.

@manonly
.SH "EXAMPLES"
@endmanonly
@snippet doc_reference_path.cpp path group
*/
